{
  "beruf": "hotel",
  "title": "Hotels and tourism",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "spanish-for-work run, 2026-11-07: written by hand for Spain, read once by an AI second reader, not yet by a human. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon or in another deck are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Tourism is one of Spain's biggest employers, from hotels on the coast to city apartments. Much of it is seasonal. This deck has reception and rooms, the work of a camarera de pisos, bookings, safety and cleaning, and what to say to guests and your team. Use usted with guests unless they say otherwise.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in a hotel or in tourism in Spain? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Camarera/o de pisos",
    "Recepcionista",
    "Botones",
    "Limpiador/a de apartamentos turísticos",
    "Mozo/a de equipajes",
    "Animador/a turístico/a"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "At reception, a smile and four sentences go a long way: «Bienvenido», «¿Tiene reserva?», «¿Me deja su DNI o su pasaporte?», «Su habitación es la 214».",
    "Knock and say «Servicio de habitaciones» or «Limpieza» before you open a door, and wait.",
    "If a guest speaks English, you can answer in English, but you still need Spanish for your team and the paperwork.",
    "In many tourist areas the contract is fijo discontinuo: you work the season and return the next. The details are in your contract and the local agreement. Ask a union or a gestoría. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
